# TypeScript & Code Quality Standards

## TypeScript Configuration
- Use strict TypeScript settings as defined in [tsconfig.json](mdc:tsconfig.json)
- Enable `strict: true`, `forceConsistentCasingInFileNames: true`
- Use path mapping with `@/*` alias for src directory imports
- Target ES5 with modern library support

## Import & Export Patterns

### Import Order
1. React and Next.js imports
2. External library imports
3. Internal library/utility imports (`@/lib/*`)
4. Component imports (`@/components/*`)
5. Type-only imports (use `import type`)

```typescript
import { useState } from 'react';
import { NextPage } from 'next';
import { zodResolver } from '@hookform/resolvers/zod';
import { getApiClient } from '@/lib/api';
import { Button } from '@/components/ui/button';
import type { User } from '@/lib/models/User';
```

### Export Patterns
- Use named exports for components and utilities
- Use default exports only for pages and layout components
- Export types separately when needed

```typescript
export const UserCard = () => { ... };
export type UserCardProps = { ... };
export default function UserPage() { ... } // Pages only
```

## Type Definitions

### Interface vs Type
- Use `interface` for object shapes that might be extended
- Use `type` for unions, intersections, and computed types
- Prefer `interface` for component props

```typescript
// Good
interface ButtonProps extends React.ButtonHTMLAttributes<HTMLButtonElement> {
  variant?: 'primary' | 'secondary';
}

// Good
type Status = 'pending' | 'approved' | 'rejected';
type ApiResponse<T> = { data: T; error?: string };
```

### Prop Types
- Always type component props explicitly
- Use generic constraints where appropriate
- Prefer readonly for props that shouldn't be mutated

```typescript
interface UserListProps {
  readonly users: User[];
  readonly onUserSelect: (user: User) => void;
  readonly loading?: boolean;
}
```

## Function Definitions

### Function Declaration Styles
- Use arrow functions for components and simple functions
- Use function declarations for complex utilities
- Use async/await over Promises

```typescript
// Components
export const UserCard = ({ user, onEdit }: UserCardProps) => {
  return <div>{user.name}</div>;
};

// API functions
export const getUsers = async (params: GetUsersParams): Promise<User[]> => {
  const client = await getApiClient();
  const response = await client.get('/users', { params });
  return response.data;
};
```

### Generic Functions
- Use descriptive generic names
- Provide constraints when necessary
- Use default generic values when appropriate

```typescript
export const createApiFunction = <TRequest, TResponse = any>(
  endpoint: string
) => {
  return async (data: TRequest): Promise<TResponse> => {
    // implementation
  };
};
```

## Code Quality Standards

### Naming Conventions
- Use PascalCase for components, interfaces, types, and classes
- Use camelCase for functions, variables, and methods
- Use SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE for constants
- Use kebab-case for file names

```typescript
// Good
const API_ENDPOINTS = {
  USERS: '/users',
  AUTH: '/auth'
} as const;

interface UserPreferences {
  readonly theme: 'light' | 'dark';
}

const getUserPreferences = async () => { ... };
```

### Error Handling
- Use try/catch blocks for async operations
- Create typed error classes when needed
- Use Result types for operations that can fail

```typescript
// API error handling
try {
  const users = await getUsers();
  return { success: true, data: users };
} catch (error) {
  console.error('Failed to fetch users:', error);
  return { success: false, error: 'Failed to fetch users' };
}
```

### Performance Considerations
- Use `React.memo` for expensive components
- Use `useCallback` and `useMemo` judiciously
- Prefer server components when possible
- Use proper TypeScript utility types

```typescript
// Use utility types
type CreateUserRequest = Pick<User, 'email' | 'name'>;
type UserUpdate = Partial<Pick<User, 'name' | 'email'>>;

// Memoized component
export const UserList = React.memo<UserListProps>(({ users, onSelect }) => {
  const sortedUsers = useMemo(
    () => users.sort((a, b) => a.name.localeCompare(b.name)),
    [users]
  );
  
  return (
    <div>
      {sortedUsers.map(user => (
        <UserCard key={user.id} user={user} onSelect={onSelect} />
      ))}
    </div>
  );
});
```

## ESLint & Prettier Integration
- Follow Next.js ESLint configuration
- Use Prettier for consistent formatting
- Prefer single quotes for strings
- Use trailing commas
- Set line length to 80-100 characters

## Documentation Standards
- Use TSDoc comments for public APIs
- Document complex types and interfaces
- Include examples for utility functions

```typescript
/**
 * Fetches user data with pagination and filtering
 * @param params - Query parameters for filtering users
 * @returns Promise resolving to paginated user data
 * @example
 * const users = await getUsers({ page: 1, limit: 10, search: 'john' });
 */
export const getUsers = async (params: GetUsersParams): Promise<PaginatedUsers> => {
  // implementation
};
```